<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import PeekDemo from "$ui/demos/sockets/PeekDemo.abide"
  import ChunksDemo from "$ui/demos/sockets/ChunksDemo.abide"
  import DoneDemo from "$ui/demos/sockets/DoneDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Reactive probes</h1>
  <p class="lead">A <a href="/sockets">socket</a> is a <a href="/caching/cell">cell</a> over a live topic, so it carries the same reactive read surface — the <strong>probes</strong> — as any cell, adapted to a stream: <code>peek()</code> is the latest message, <code>chunks()</code> is the tail transcript, <code>done()</code> reports whether the topic is idle (no live subscription), and <code>pending()</code>/<code>refreshing()</code>/<code>error()</code> report connection status. Same vocabulary as the <a href="/caching/probes">generic-cell</a> and <a href="/rpc/probes">RPC</a> probes. The <code>peek</code>/<code>chunks</code> demos share the one <code>socketsChat</code> topic, so a publish anywhere updates both.</p>

  <h2><code>peek()</code> — the latest message</h2>
  <p>The most recent message on the topic, reactive, with no list to maintain. Reading it opens the subscription, so it updates live as messages arrive.</p>
  <Demo id="socket-peek" title="<code>socketsChat.peek()</code>" server="src/server/sockets/socketsChat.ts" client="src/ui/demos/sockets/PeekDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <PeekDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2><code>chunks()</code> — the tail transcript</h2>
  <p>The recent-message tail as a snapshot array (capped by the socket's <code>tail</code>). Render it directly with a plain <code>{'{'}#for{'}'}</code> instead of appending in a <code>{'{'}#for await{'}'}</code> — same data, snapshot rather than stream.</p>
  <Demo id="socket-chunks" title="<code>socketsChat.chunks()</code>" server="src/server/sockets/socketsChat.ts" client="src/ui/demos/sockets/ChunksDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <ChunksDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2><code>done()</code> — idle vs live</h2>
  <p>A status probe over the subscription lifecycle: <strong>yes</strong> while the topic is idle — no active read holds a subscription — and <strong>no</strong> once a live read opens one. It uses a standalone topic (untouched by the demos above) so it starts idle; open a subscription and it flips.</p>
  <Demo id="socket-done" title="<code>socketsPulse.done()</code>" server="src/server/sockets/socketsPulse.ts" client="src/ui/demos/sockets/DoneDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <DoneDemo />
  </Demo>
</main>
